  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The fire-tea had gone cold in both cups.
    
    Gilt-Hand Yeaiu qi'Gleam sat in her usual spot at the eastern edge of Terrace Momiou's morning market, watching the merchants avoid looking at her. Twenty cycles of this—setting out two cups, drinking one, leaving the other to cool until the vendors packed up and someone quiet disposed of it. The second cup had become a kind of memorial. Zheishongfeihua hadn't come since the schism, since Yeaiu signed the Long-dark Edict and authenticated it with betrayal, but hope was a stupid creature that didn't know when to stop breathing.
    
    The aurora-thread wrapped around her left flipper didn't gleam anymore. It used to, back when she and Zheishongfeihua first chartered the E'frostlings together, back when the Compass of Crystal recognized her touch as worthy. Now the silver thread sat dull against her dark feathers, a binding that had become a brand.
    
    "The ice has grown colder beneath the middle gallery." The voice came from behind her—scratched low, worn smooth by decades of speaking to students who wouldn't listen. "The corruption pools where the fire-cores once burned clean."
    
    Yeaiu didn't turn. She knew that voice. Had trained under it, argued with it, fled from it when the schism tore everything apart. "Umbra-Princeps. You're far from the tunnels."
    
    Alnae~ Oss moved into view, and Yeaiu saw the bone cage at her throat—the Dreaming Tooth wrapped in ash-gray thread, its luminescence bleeding through like a wound that wouldn't close. Her flippers bore leather wraps that couldn't quite hide the ice-burn patterns beneath. "The tunnels have grown crowded with shadows that speak back. The Covens—" She stopped. Started again. "The Order I founded has forgotten what it was founded for."
    
    "Mandatum∴absconditu."
    
    "Worse. Spite wearing the mask of purpose." Alnae~ Oss settled beside her, not looking at the second cup. "They guard fire-cores with the devotion of priests and can't remember why fire matters. They read the frozen memory-scars and see only what they wish to see."
    
    The market continued around them—merchants calling prices, fire-core shipments being weighed and argued over, the careful dance of the Flipper Accord keeping two civilizations from each other's throats. Yeaiu watched a young vendor set aside the best fire-cores for her, the way they always did. A courtesy that cost nothing. Meant everything.
    
    "Why are you here, Alnae~?"
    
    "The Compass requires reforging." The words fell like ice cracking. "Twelve segments served the old age. Thirteen where twelve once stood—that's what the frozen memories show, in the deep places where the ice still remembers clearly. But there's something else. Something buried at Terrace Momiou that spreads faster than the sanctification rituals can contain."
    
    "The scorch-marks in the middle gallery. From the Vum∴tenebra."
    
    "Not from the war." Alnae~ Oss's voice dropped lower. "From what we buried afterward. Experimental work. The Covens tried to fuse aurora-light with captured flame—create something that burned eternal and never needed feeding." Her scarred flippers clutched her cloak against the wind. "It worked. For three days it worked beautifully."
    
    "Then?"
    
    "Then it learned to hunger."
    
    The cold fire-tea sat between them, steam long since vanished.
    
    "The Compass navigates toward it," Alnae~ Oss continued. "The corruption we pretend doesn't exist—it calls to the artifact like a key seeking its lock. If we're going to reforge the Compass into something that can guide the new age, we have to face what feeds on the old one first."
    
    Yeaiu looked at the empty seat where Zheishongfeihua should have been, cycle after cycle, year after year. "She won't help me."
    
    "Then we retrieve the Compass first and make her understand after. Twenty years is long enough to mourn a friendship. The ice remembers what we sacrifice—it doesn't ask us to keep sacrificing forever." Alnae~ Oss stood. "The vault lies beneath the eastern spire. Sealed three times over. You signed one of those seals."
    
    She had. The memory sat in her chest like a stone: her gilt-hand pressing against the still-warm crystal, the authentication glow flickering because even then, even before Zheishongfeihua stopped coming, the magic itself recoiled from what she'd done.
    
    "When?"
    
    "Now. Before the ☽'shadow Covens' Order remembers what they're guarding and why they shouldn't let anyone take it." Alnae~ Oss's silhouette was already moving toward the eastern stairs. "Coming, student?"
    
    Student. She hadn't been called that in longer than she'd set out two cups of tea.
    
    Yeaiu left the cold cup where it sat and followed.
    
    ---
    
    The middle gallery smelled like regret.
    
    That wasn't poetry—something in the scorched crystal held old griefs, emotional residue frozen into the walls during The Vum∴tenebra. The war had been over since before Yeaiu was hatched, but the ice remembered, and what it remembered was loss.
    
    Alnae~ Oss moved through the shadows with the certainty of someone who'd walked these paths when they still blazed. "The scorch-marks aren't random," she said quietly. "They spiral inward toward the sealed gallery. The fire-core we buried—it pulls at everything. Light. Heat. Memory. We lost fourteen fire-keepers trying to contain it before we admitted defeat and sealed everything away."
    
    "The peace we negotiated," Yeaiu said. "The Long-dark Edict."
    
    "Bought time to forget what lived here. Yes." Alnae~ Oss paused at a junction where three tunnels met, each marked with authentication glyphs from different factions. "This way. Quietly."
    
    The eastern vault's entrance had been carved by E'frostlings, long before the ☽'shadow Covens' Order existed—elegant spirals of aurora-crystal that caught even the faint light bleeding down from above. Yeaiu remembered charting trade routes through these passages with Zheishongfeihua, the two of them laughing at how serious the old factions took their borders. That laughter felt like something that had happened to someone else.
    
    "Hold." Alnae~ Oss's flipper shot out, stopping her.
    
    Figures in the tunnel ahead. Three of them, wrapped in the ash-gray cloaks of Coven sentries, their eyes tracking something that wasn't there. One murmured to the empty air; another nodded at a response no one else could hear.
    
    Touched. Corruption spreading through the Order like smoke through tunnels.
    
    "They guard a fire-core cache," Alnae~ Oss breathed. "But they've forgotten what fire is for. Only that it must be kept."
    
    "Can we go around?"
    
    "No." The Umbra-Princeps's scarred flippers gripped her cloak. "But we don't need to. Watch."
    
    She stepped forward, into the torchlight, and the three sentries turned as one. Yeaiu's heart stopped—but their faces showed no recognition of threat. Only reverence.
    
    "Umbra-Princeps." The lead sentry's voice was hollow, distant. "You've come back."
    
    "I never left," Alnae~ Oss said gently. "I'm only walking the old ways. Checking the fire-cores. Making sure they burn clean."
    
    The sentry nodded, satisfied by an answer that meant nothing, and stepped aside. The others followed, their attention already drifting back to whatever voices called them from the corruption's edge.
    
    Yeaiu waited until they were past, then whispered: "They didn't question—"
    
    "They can't question. The Mandatum∴absconditu ate that from them." Alnae~ Oss didn't slow. "Spite wears the mask of purpose, student. Remember that. They believe they serve something greater because believing nothing would crack them open entirely."
    
    The vault door loomed ahead—three seals overlapping, three factions' distrust crystallized into warding light. The E'frostlings' mark. The Covens' mark.
    
    And hers.
    
    Yeaiu's gilt-hand began to glow.
    
    ---
    
    The vault opened onto a throat of crystalline darkness.
    
    The Compass of Crystal waited on its pedestal, twelve segments catching the faint glow from Yeaiu's gilt-hand. But beyond it, through an archway that should have been sealed, the corridor continued—spiraling downward toward light that flickered between aurora-blue and furnace-orange, never settling, never stable.
    
    "The gallery seal failed," Alnae~ Oss breathed. "The fire-core—"
    
    The Compass blazed. Its segments cycled in sequence—third, seventh, ninth, first—pulling toward what lay below. The hairline fracture from third to seventh pulsed with light that shouldn't exist in glass that old, that damaged. Zheishongfeihua's restoration was failing.
    
    And the fire-core was calling.
    
    "We take the Compass and leave," Yeaiu said. "Now, before—"
    
    "Before what?" Alnae~ Oss's voice carried the weight of someone who had spent too many years navigating darkness. "Before it grows stronger? Before the corruption spreads further? This is why the Compass led us here, student. Not just to retrieve it—to face what it navigates toward."
    
    The gallery opened before them like a wound. The scorch-marks spiraled inward toward a crater in the floor where crystal had melted and reformed in patterns that hurt to perceive. At the center: a fire-core the size of a penguin's head, pulsing with light that couldn't decide what it wanted to be.
    
    Beautiful. Obscene. Hungry.
    
    "It feeds on what we refuse to acknowledge," Alnae~ Oss said. "Our betrayals. Our convenient blindness. The costs we pretend didn't matter because we survived to trade another day." She looked at Yeaiu. "That's why you're here. The Compass doesn't navigate toward safety. It navigates toward truth."
    
    The fire-core pulsed. Recognized. Reached.
    
    Darkness poured forth—not shadow, but absence, a thing that existed by consuming what surrounded it. It touched the walls and ice-memory shattered into screaming static. The Compass blazed in Yeaiu's grip, segments aligning toward the corrupt flame like a key finding its lock.
    
    And Alnae~ Oss stepped between them.
    
    Her scarred flippers unwrapped for the first time, and Yeaiu saw the ice-burn patterns weren't random—they were words, navigation markers scorched into flesh by repeated contact with memories that shouldn't be touched.
    
    "Take the Compass!" Alnae~ Oss pressed her burned flippers to the gallery wall. "I'll hold!"
    
    "There has to be another way—"
    
    "There's always another way, student. But some paths only open when you stop going around the darkness and go *through* it." The Dreaming Tooth blazed at her throat. "Third path. Remember that."
    
    She pushed.
    
    The gallery screamed—not with sound, but with image. The Vum∴tenebra blazed again in frozen echo, fire-and-light searing through corridors in memory that was almost real. The corrupted fire-core recoiled, confusion replacing hunger as it faced acknowledgment instead of denial—truth offered instead of truth hidden.
    
    Alnae~ Oss's life bled out through the burn-scars on her flippers, every navigation lesson she'd ever taught paying out in one terrible burst. The darkness that poured from the fire-core touched her and stopped. Tasted something different. Richer. More sustaining than convenient blindness had ever been.
    
    Her mouth moved, shaping words she no longer had breath for:
    
    *Light stored against the Long Dark.*
    
    The corrupted fire-core dimmed. Not destroyed—still hungry, still dangerous—but fed on something it couldn't corrupt. Acknowledged debt instead of denied cost.
    
    Yeaiu took the Compass and ran.
    
    ---
    
    Zheishongfeihua's restoration chamber lay beneath Aurora Stack's deepest vault, where no faction war had ever reached.
    
    Yeaiu found her there, surrounded by tools she recognized from decades past—the aurora-glass prisms, the silver-ice needles, the careful apparatus for channeling sky-light into crystal. Zheishongfeihua looked up when the chamber's light shifted, and her eyes—the same eyes that had stopped meeting Yeaiu's at market mornings, that had turned away rather than acknowledge the ghost who kept hoping—went wide.
    
    "You shouldn't have this."
    
    "Alnae~ Oss is dead." The words tasted like cold fire-tea. "The ice beneath Terrace Momiou holds her now. She bought time with her life, and I will not waste the purchase."
    
    Zheishongfeihua said nothing. Her flippers—ungilded, unmarked by silver thread or magic's recoil—trembled at her sides.
    
    "The corruption still lives," Yeaiu continued. "The fire-core we buried—it learned to hunger, and we fed it for twenty years with everything we refused to acknowledge. Twelve segments aren't enough to navigate what's coming." She held out the Compass, and the aurora-thread on her gilt-hand began to glow—faint, but real. The first gleam in twenty years. "You restored it to what it was. Help me restore it to what it must become."
    
    "Thirteen where twelve once stood." Zheishongfeihua's voice was barely audible. "The deep memories showed me that. But I couldn't—the fracture required—"
    
    "Someone who carries the weight of both peoples. I know." Yeaiu set the Compass on the workbench between them. "I betrayed you. I betrayed the E'frostlings. I authenticated the Long-dark Edict because I was afraid of what would happen if I didn't, and I've set out two cups of fire-tea every morning since because hoping you'd forgive me was easier than deserving it."
    
    Her gilt-hand pressed to the Compass, and the glow intensified.
    
    "Alnae~ called it the third path," Yeaiu said quietly. "Not going around the darkness. Going through it. The thirteenth segment—it's not just navigation for a new age. It's navigation for the costs we carry. The debts that can never be repaid, only acknowledged."
    
    Zheishongfeihua looked at her for what felt like the first time in two decades. "The fracture from third to seventh. You want to keep it."
    
    "I want to make it deliberate. History can't be erased—but it can become the path forward instead of the wound that won't heal." Yeaiu's voice cracked. "I'm not asking for forgiveness. I'm asking for help."
    
    The Compass began to warm. Not uncomfortably—but enough that she noticed her flipper-pads sweating, the way they always had, the way Zheishongfeihua's hands had noticed all those years ago when they first charted trade routes together.
    
    Zheishongfeihua reached for the prisms.
    
    "I'll need aurora-light. The brightest we can channel."
    
    "The sky's clear tonight."
    
    "Then we work until dawn." Her flippers were already moving—adjusting angles, positioning needles, preparing the apparatus for something more than restoration. "If you're staying."
    
    Yeaiu stayed.
    
    ---
    
    The aurora blazed.
    
    It poured through the crystal spires of Aurora Stack like water through a breaking dam—twenty years of stored light flooding into the chamber, into the prisms, into the Compass of Crystal where it lay fractured and waiting to be reborn. The twelve segments caught fire; the hairline crack from third to seventh became deliberate channels, rivers of brilliance carving new pathways through the frozen glass.
    
    Zheishongfeihua's flippers moved with precision that wasn't hers alone. Something greater guided her—the accumulated weight of every penguin who'd ever needed navigation, every soul who'd trusted the Compass to show them the way.
    
    "The thirteenth segment." Her voice was strained. "It won't form unless—"
    
    "I know." Yeaiu pressed her gilt-hand to the Compass's frame, and the aurora-thread that had been dull for so long blazed silver-white. The pain was extraordinary—magic recoiling, authenticating, *accepting*. "The ice remembers what we sacrifice. Let it remember this."
    
    She thought of Alnae~ Oss, pressing burned flippers to the gallery wall. Of the fire-core that learned to hunger, and the truth that could make it recede. Of twenty years of cold tea poured for ghosts, and the path that led through breaking instead of around it.
    
    The chamber shook. Aurora Stack's deepest vault groaned with the weight of miracle.
    
    And beneath their combined touch, the Compass of Crystal bloomed.
    
    Thirteen segments caught the light—one more than the old world knew. The fractures had become facets; the cracks, deliberate beauty. When Yeaiu lifted it, the warmth that spread through her flipper wasn't painful anymore. It was recognition.
    
    The new age had a navigator.
    
    Outside, above the wounded terraces of Momiou, the aurora pulsed patterns that the Touched couldn't corrupt and the hardliners couldn't deny. Alnae~ Oss's sacrifice crystallized into the deep ice, her final lesson preserved for those who would learn to read it: *Light stored against the Long Dark.*
    
    In the deepest gallery, the corrupted fire-core still pulsed. Still hungry. Still dangerous. But smaller now, feeding on acknowledged debt instead of denied cost—shrinking toward something that might, someday, be safely held.
    
    Zheishongfeihua's flipper brushed Yeaiu's—the ungilded touching the gilt, the estranged reaching across twenty years of silence.
    
    "Tomorrow," the Assessor said quietly. "I'll come to market."
    
    Yeaiu looked at her bare flipper, the scars that formed their own navigation, and decided not to wrap them anymore. Let the ice witness. Let the aurora judge. Let the world see what survival cost and call it by its true name.
    
    Two cups of fire-tea.
    
    Both of them warm.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771578580789_0", anchor: "the careful dance of the Flipper Accord keeping two civilizations from each other's throats", text: "The chronicler uses this as scenery. It is not scenery. Fire-core shipments being weighed on a terrace built for crystal commerce, under an accord renegotiated three times in secret before the version anyone remembers — this is the load-bearing structure of everything that follows. Remove it and both civilizations freeze or starve within the season. The Accord does not appear again in this narrative. It did not need to. It is the floor.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578580789_1", anchor: "the Dreaming Tooth wrapped in ash-gray thread, its luminescence bleeding through like a wound that wouldn't close", text: "The Tooth's unsolicited luminescence, sentries conversing with unseen presences, ice-memory replaying the Vum∴tenebra as 'frozen echo' vivid enough to stagger a corrupted fire-core — these are thin-veil conditions. The chronicler never names the Long Dark, but every symptom is here: enhanced perception, boundary erosion, the dead speaking more clearly than expected. Either this occurs during or adjacent to a Long Dark, or the corrupted fire-core has locally reproduced thin-veil phenomena by destabilizing the boundary between memory and present. The latter possibility is worse.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771578580789_2", anchor: "We lost fourteen fire-keepers trying to contain it", text: "Fourteen. Not 'roughly a dozen,' not 'many.' Fourteen is the kind of number that survives because someone counted bodies. I'll allow it.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578580789_3", anchor: "The Covens tried to fuse aurora-light with captured flame", text: "This is not experimental. The deep ice at stratum 3 contains impressions of aurora-fire fusion attempts predating any faction the chronicler names — pre-Faction Wars, possibly pre-Great Thaw. The geometry of the failure patterns is identical. What the Covens did was rediscover a catastrophe and mistake it for innovation. The ice has opinions about this, and I share them.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771578580789_4", anchor: "The Mandatum∴absconditu ate that from them", text: "Consistent with my prior annotation: the plague enacts the doctrine's logic. These sentries are its late-stage expression — functional, reverent, unable to distinguish hidden truth from visible truth. The chronicler frames this as tragedy. It is. But it is also diagnosis.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578580789_5", anchor: "Alnae~ Oss's life bled out through the burn-scars on her flippers", text: "The account has Alnae~ Oss single-handedly holding back a corruption event through personal sacrifice while her student escapes with the artifact. I have read this story before — about the Orca Incursion, about the Faction Wars, about every moment where institutional failure needed a hero to absorb the blame the institution deserved. The cast details confirm she died beneath Terrace Momiou, consumed by the corrupted fire-core. They do not confirm she was alone, or that the Nightshelf intelligence networks coordinating every other significant action in these tunnels were somehow absent from this one.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771578580789_6", anchor: "beneath Aurora Stack's deepest vault, where no faction war had ever reached", text: "It reached. Stratum 4 administrative ice contains sealed records from two separate faction incursions into the deep vaults. 'Never reached' means no faction war anyone currently in power wishes to remember. The distinction matters to those of us who work there.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
